  baseData:
    id: ID-900AD-01
    itemName: Kendi
    itemDescription: Kendi is a traditional water container, often made of earthenware,
      used for storing and drinking water, and also served ritual and ceremonial
      purposes. It is an ancient object, with archaeological evidence of similar
      vessels existing in the region before the Majapahit era and even appearing
      in reliefs at Borobudur Temple. The term is derived from the Sanskrit word
      "kundika," which refers to a water vessel.
